    "description": "Television production in Iran developed through commercial broadcasting, national television, and institutions connecting television with cinema. Today its study also requires distinguishing broadcast production from programmes made for internet distribution and examining how commissioning and regulation shape creative work.",
    "tags": ["Television production", "Iranian television", "Screenwriting", "Streaming", "Media institutions"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:18:19Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:18:19Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Television Production in Iran: Institutions, Drama and Streaming",
      "description": "Understand the history of Iranian television production, its connections with cinema and literary adaptation, and the emergence of internet-distributed series."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "early-production",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Building a production culture",
        "content": "Television broadcasting began in Iran through private enterprise in 1958. Early schedules combined imported material with locally made programmes, while a shortage of trained staff constrained production. National television subsequently expanded the institutional base for making programmes. This history matters because television production did not begin as a single, fully developed state industry: equipment, skills, scheduling, and organizational authority developed together. [s1]\n\nThe boundary between television and cinema was particularly permeable. Encyclopaedia Iranica records the establishment of a College for Television and Film in 1966 and describes Telfilm, a production entity associated with national broadcasting. Its work included shorts, feature films, and serials. Broadcaster-supported production therefore contributed to a screen culture extending beyond material designed solely for a television schedule. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "literary-adaptation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Literature becomes television",
        "content": "My Uncle Napoleon offers a clear example of television’s relationship with literature. Iraj Pezeshkzad’s satirical novel became a television series directed by Nasser Taqvai in the 1970s. Stanford’s Iranian Studies programme describes its characters and story as enduring cultural reference points. The narrative follows a Tehran family dominated by a patriarch whose conspiratorial interpretation of events supplies much of the satire. [s3]\n\nFor a reader encountering Iranian television for the first time, adaptation provides a useful entry point. It connects production choices with an existing literary world: characters, domestic space, social hierarchy, and spoken humour. The series also illustrates why a history of television cannot be reduced to a list of channels. A particular production can have a cultural identity that outlasts the institutional setting in which it first appeared. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "commissioning",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Who makes a programme, and who can show it?",
        "content": "Islamic Republic of Iran Broadcasting, or IRIB, combines broadcasting with substantial internal production. Persheng Vaziri’s research also identifies programmes supplied by other state-connected institutions and work made by independent producers for IRIB channels. Production is consequently best understood through the relationships between the commissioning body, producer, filmmaker, and broadcaster. [s4]\n\nVaziri documents cases in which completed documentaries faced demands for changes or were not broadcast. Commissioning, completion, and transmission are therefore distinct events. A credit identifying a television sponsor does not, by itself, prove that the finished film reached a television audience in the version its director intended. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "internet-distribution",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Production beyond the broadcast schedule",
        "content": "Internet distribution adds another institutional setting. Filterwatch’s 2021 research describes private businesses offering internet-protocol television, over-the-top services, and video on demand, including exclusive programming. These terms concern different ways of delivering or selecting audiovisual content; they should not automatically be treated as equivalent to terrestrial television networks. [s5]\n\nThe same research identifies disputes over IRIB’s regulatory role and the place of private platforms within a state-sanctioned media system. It establishes a history of contention, rather than a definitive statement of every rule in force at the research cutoff. [s5]\n\nA useful assessment of any Iranian production therefore asks three concrete questions: who financed or commissioned it, where it was intended to appear, and which version was actually released. Those distinctions connect creative achievement with the institutional conditions that make it visible. [s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
